Spot-audits and precision/recall evaluation catch fundamentally different failure modes, which is why both are necessary for a trustworthy extraction pipeline. Gold-set evaluation tells you whether the model gets the right label on a fixed set of annotated examples, producing aggregate statistics like F1 across event types. Spot-auditing involves a human reading the raw text alongside the extracted output, checking not just whether the label matches but whether the evidence and confidence are genuinely grounded in the document. A failure mode that gold-set evaluation alone would miss is systematic defaulting to a safe label on ambiguous documents: if the model outputs 
\fs26 other
\fs24  whenever a text is unclear, it may look acceptable on a balanced gold set but silently fail on the majority of real-world documents that are noisy or underspecified. A failure mode that random spot-auditing alone would miss is consistent misclassification of a rare event type: if 
\fs26 violence
\fs24  events make up only five percent of the corpus, a random sample of ten documents may include none, so the auditor never sees that the model has recall of zero for that class. Precision/recall on a deliberately balanced gold set would surface this immediately as a missing category in the classification report. Together, the two methods cover both aggregate performance and the qualitative plausibility of individual extractions.\
